import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from con_helpers import *
save(2,3,"The I-Maker: Ahaṁkāra and the Volume Knob","This week, when something stings, spot the stamp: my failure, my fault, about me. Then find the volume knob and ask: how loud is this turned up, and does it need to be?",[
mc("U","Which three jobs does the lesson give ahaṁkāra, the I-maker?",
 ["Self-reference, appropriation and differentiation: a centre, a claim of mine, a me/not-me boundary.","Perception, decision and memory: seeing what arrives, resolving its meaning and storing the trace.","Attention, judgement and recall: noticing a signal, weighing it and bringing back an old one.","Desire, fear and pride: wanting what is pleasant, avoiding what is not and defending what is mine."],0,
 "Kai’s online comparison shows self-reference: a me is always being measured. James’s lesson that wobbles becomes my failure through appropriation. Sarah’s it is raining versus my picnic is ruined shows the boundary: this side is me and mine, to be protected.",
 "Self-reference, appropriation, differentiation","Part 1"),
mc("A","Sarah feels guilty about having an ego at all. What does Swamiji say?",
 ["Do not: without a working I she could not keep a promise or learn, and the aim is to see through the ego.","Do: the ego is an enemy that has invaded her, and the task of practice is to remove it altogether from her.","Do not worry: the ego is not real, so there is nothing for her to see through and nothing to be done at all.","Do: guilt is the proper response, since the ego is a spiritual fault that every learner has to overcome in time."],0,
 "A toddler learning to say I want and that’s mine is building a necessary structure. In the Kashmir tradition the ego is the same infinite awareness appearing in a narrowed, identified form, so she is not broken for having one.",
 "The ego is needed; the invitation is to see through it","Part 1"),
judge("“The ego becomes a problem at one precise moment: when the functional ‘I’ becomes the absolute ‘I,’ as in ‘I made a mistake’ hardening into ‘I am a failure.’”",T,
 "Then the ego stops being a tool you use and becomes the whole of what you take yourself to be, and everything is filtered through self-defence, attachment, fear, pride, shame and comparison. I do this job hardening into I am this job is the same step.",
 "The problem is absolute identification","Part 1"),
mc("A","Two teachers get the same note: “your lesson plans need more structure.” Teacher A is devastated; Teacher B adjusts. What explains it?",
 ["Ahaṁkāra amplifies what is tagged me, like a volume knob: A is her teaching; B only does teaching.","Teacher A has worse lesson plans than teacher B, so the note was fairer and harder for A to hear.","Teacher B does not care about teaching, so the note could not hurt, and nothing in it was taken to heart by B.","Teacher A has a stronger ego, so only a person with a small ego can take a note without being hurt at all."],0,
 "Same signal, wildly different suffering. You usually cannot control what happens to you, but you can lower the gain; working on the volume is often far more effective than rearranging the world. It works on the good side too: a child whose whole worth rides on a gold star is as captured as one crushed by a poor mark.",
 "The volume knob on everything tagged me","Part 2"),
mc("A","Sarah has spent years trying to arrange life so nothing hurts her children. What does Swamiji suggest teaching them instead?",
 ["That when it hurts, it does not define you: lowering the gain, as no one can promise nothing will hurt.","That nothing will ever hurt them, since a parent who works hard enough can arrange the world to be safe for them.","That hurt should be hidden, since a child who shows pain is letting the pain define them in front of other people.","That hurt is only imagined, since a child who thinks clearly will see that nothing real has happened to them."],0,
 "Swamiji calls this one of the kindest things a parent can teach. Helping them turn the volume down means the hurt that does come does not become I am unlovable.",
 "When it hurts, it does not get to define you","Part 2"),
mc("A","Kai says his problem is not arrogance but feeling he is not enough, and asks whether that is really ego. What does Swamiji answer?",
 ["“I am worthless” is as much ego as “I am superior”: one machine on different fuel, a defended self-image.","“I am worthless” is humility, since a person who thinks little of themselves has little ego left in them at all.","“I am worthless” is a fact, since a person who feels it must have seen something true about themselves there.","“I am worthless” is not ego, since ego is the sense of being better than others, and nothing more than that."],0,
 "The grandiose ego shouts I am the best; the timid ego whispers I am the worst, but both have a self-image at the centre with the volume up. Self-criticism is the ego pointed inward. The problem is not the size of the ego but the absoluteness of the identification.",
 "Self-criticism is the ego pointed inward","Part 3"),
mc("A","Sarah’s husband says nine kind things and one careless one, and she chews on the careless one at midnight. Which worker does Swamiji say the I-maker is bending here?",
 ["Manas, the attention, which it steers toward the self-relevant, threatening bit.","Buddhi, the interpreter, which it makes read an ambiguous text as what did I do wrong.","Chitta, the memory, which it turns into a story like I am always the one left out.","None of them, since the I-maker acts only on its own and cannot lean on the other workers."],0,
 "Swamiji says it leans on all of them. It biases buddhi toward the dark reading of an ambiguous text, and bends chitta into a story that recruits evidence for itself, so every new event is filed under it and the story comes true by selection.",
 "The I-maker biases attention toward threat","Part 3"),
mc("A","James has a student who cannot take in an A: “you are just being nice.” What does the lesson say about this?",
 ["Ahaṁkāra’s last defence: it filters out what contradicts the self-image, keeping out help as well as attack.","Stubbornness, which the student could overcome at once by deciding to accept the evidence of the mark itself.","Lack of intelligence, since a student who understood the mark would see that the praise is deserved by them.","A fault of the teacher, since a teacher who praised in just the right way would be believed at once by the student."],0,
 "This is why insight alone rarely heals: the defences themselves have to soften, and they soften not by force but by being gently, repeatedly seen. James is to name it kindly to himself and offer praise as plain fact, lightly, with no pressure to accept it.",
 "The fortress keeps out help as well as attack","Part 3"),
judge("“Research on the self supports the lesson: the self is a process the brain builds, and clinical psychology sees ego trouble in fragile self-esteem and shame as well as in arrogance.”",T,
 "Researchers describe a self-model updated by the body, memory and social feedback, and find that a threat to identity lights up the system much like a threat to the body, which is why being contradicted can feel like being attacked. Two roads, the lab and the meditation seat, arrive at the same place: the everyday I is assembled, useful and not the final truth of you.",
 "Science and the traditions converge on the assembled I","Part 3"),
mc("A","Kai’s comparison online bites. What does the practical experiment ask him to do?",
 ["Not fight or moralise, but locate the knob: “the am I enough volume is high now,” and let it settle.","Win the argument with the feeling, so that it has nothing left to say and can be put away for good and all.","Switch off the phone for a week, so that the comparing has nothing to feed on and the volume falls away.","Tell himself that he is enough, since a firm and cheerful statement will turn the feeling off at once for him."],0,
 "Swamiji says you do not have to win the argument with the feeling, you just turn it down. The two parts are catch the stamp (my failure, my fault, about me) and find the volume knob.",
 "Find the knob, not the argument","Practical situation")])
